  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nToday and Tonight\n\nA 1003 mb surface low will slowly move southeast across \nnorthern Illinois this morning and into west-central Indiana \nthis afternoon. As this occurs, the deformation band of rain \nwill move southeast and out of the area between 01.18z and \n01.20z. Additional rainfall in southwest and central Wisconsin \nwill likely range between 0.25 to 1 inch.\n\nAnother weak shortwave trough will move through the area this \nafternoon. With 0-1 km mixed-layer CAPES up to 500 J/kg and some\nsurface convergence, isolated to scattered showers and storms \nacross north-central, west-central, and central Wisconsin. With \nweak 0-3 km and 0-6 km shear, not anticipating any organized \nsevere weather.\n\nHigh temperatures will range from the mid-70s to around 80. Low\ntemperatures tonight will range from the lower 50s to lower \n60s.\n\nSunday into Monday\n\nAn upper level ridge will build across the Upper Mississippi River \nValley on Sunday and then remain across the region through Monday. \nThe combination of this ridge and a surface high will keep the area \ndry through this time period. High temperatures will range from \naround 80 to the mid-80s. Low temperatures will range from the lower \n50s to lower 60s.\n\nMonday Night into Thursday\n\nOn Monday night and Tuesday, a shortwave trough will move along\nthe US/Canadian border. This trough will flatten the upper \nlevel ridge and this will allow a cold front to move into the \nregion late Monday night and Tuesday morning. With very limited \ninstability (less than 100 J/kg), only expecting some isolated \nweak showers and storms during this time period.\n\nThis front will then meander across the region from Tuesday \nafternoon into Thursday. As several mid-level speed maxes move \nthrough the region, the 0-3 km and 0-6 km shear may become \nfavorable at times for severe weather to develop. \n\nHigh temperatures will range from the mid-70s to mid-80s. Low \ntemperatures will range from the mid-50s to mid-60s.",
